e graph below there's a  rise of progesterone and that's really  because of the corpus luteum so in the  graph you can see around day 16 17 18  the gold line the yellow line begins to  increase in rise that's because the  corpus luteum is releasing large amounts  of progesterone and the purpose of these  hormones is if an egg is to become  fertilized then these hormones will  prepare the endometrium the lining of  the uterus in case the egg is fertilized  the lining of the mute of the uterus  will be prepared for the embryo to  implant and so this monthly process the  menstrual cycle will continue until a  woman hit a stage in in development  known as menopause and this is when the  menstrual cycle stops and usually this  happens in a woman's 40s to 50s again  it's a little different for each woman  but that's generally the time pattern  when this occurs  and the reason for menopause is that  there's a decline in these hormone  levels that we saw on the previous  slides  the LH the FSH the estrogen and the  progesterone there's a decline in these  hormones which makes the menstrual cycle  very irregular and ultimately the  menstrual cycle stops completely and at  that point there's there's no more  elating so if a woman's not ovulating  she's not having a menstrual cycle and  can no longer become pregnant some of  these symptoms and side effects of  menopause you can see in the picture so  there you go there's a hopefully a quick  introduction on the female reproductive  system if you want to pause this video  and work on these questions if you're in  my biology class you can write them down  in a separate sheet of paper and I'd be  happy to check your answers before  school or after school good luck
 

